1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
Welcome back to Hardball. We're back with the 39th President of the United States, Jimmy Carter.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,640
His new book is called Palestine, Peace, Not Apartheid.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,800
President Carter, why did you use the word apartheid in the book's title?

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,560
Well, let's look at the entire title, if you don't mind.

5
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:20,780
The first word is Palestine, which involves the land that belongs to the Palestinians, not the Israelis.

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:27,480
I didn't refer to Israel because there's no semblance of anything relating to apartheid within the nation of Israel.

7
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:32,480
And I also emphasize the word not, that is, peace and not apartheid.

8
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,060
That's what I hope to accomplish for this book is some move toward that goal.

9
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:43,480
But there's no doubt that within the occupied territories, Palestinian land, that there is a horrendous example of apartheid.

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:51,640
The occupation of Palestinian land, the confiscation of that land that doesn't belong to Israel,

11
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:54,720
the building of settlements on it, the colonization of that land,

12
00:00:54,720 --> 00:01:00,640
and then the connection of those isolated but multiple settlements, more than 200 of them,

13
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:07,820
with each other by highways on which Palestinians can't travel and quite often where Palestinians cannot even cross.

14
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:15,040
So the persecution of the Palestinians now under the occupying territories, under the occupation forces,

15
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:19,020
is one of the worst examples of human rights deprivation that I know.

16
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:22,620
Even worse, though, than a place like Rwanda?

17
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,620
Yes, I think, yes.

18
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:25,620
You mean now?

19
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:26,040
Yes.

20
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:30,180
The impression now of the Israelis, of the Palestinians by the Israelis,

21
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:35,000
is worse than a situation in Africa like the oppression of Rwandans and the Civil War?

22
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:37,980
I'm not going back into ancient history about Rwanda,

23
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:43,560
but right now the persecution of the Palestinians is one of the worst examples of human rights abuse, I know,

24
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:44,480
because the Palestinians are...

25
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:45,440
What are you talking about right now?

26
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,420
You're not talking about, say, a bit of two years ago?

27
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:48,000
I'm not talking about ancient history, no.

28
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,520
Well, Rwanda wasn't ancient history, it was just a few years ago.

29
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:52,620
You can talk about Rwanda if you want to.

30
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,040
I want to talk about Palestine.

31
00:01:55,380 --> 00:02:03,920
What is being done to the Palestinians now is horrendous in their own territory by the occupying powers, which is Israel.

32
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:10,640
They've taken away all the basic human rights of the Palestinians, as was done in South Africa against the blacks.

33
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:18,500
And I make it very plain in this book that the apartheid is not based on racism, as it was in South Africa,

34
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:26,980
but it's based on the desire of a minority of Israelis to acquire land that belongs to the Palestinians and to retain that land,

35
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:38,100
and then to exclude the Palestinians from their own property and subjugate them so that they can't arouse and demonstrate their disapproval of being robbed of their own property.

36
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:41,020
That's what's happening in the West Bank.

37
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:44,940
And the people in this country, in America, never know about this.

38
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:46,160
They never discuss this.

39
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:47,320
There's no debate about it.

40
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:50,300
There's no criticism of Israel in this country.

41
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:55,760
And in Israel, there is an intense debate about the issues in this book.

42
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:56,900
In this country, no.

43
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:57,440
I agree.

44
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:59,520
I mean, I wish we had that sort of debate that they're having in Israel.

45
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:00,720
I wish we had that in the United States.

46
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:02,220
But give us a sort of sense.

47
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:12,140
How much of the responsibility for the conflict, the Israeli-Palestine conflict, do you think belongs to the Israelis for their tactics like seizing land and occupying territory that didn't belong to them?

48
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:17,880
How much of it is the responsibility of the Palestinians for their suicide terror attacks and their bombings within Israel proper?

49
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:27,880
Well, as a matter of fact, the basic cause of the conflict is a sustained occupation of other people's land by the Israelis.

50
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:31,900
And this is a direct violation of the United Nations resolutions.

51
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:35,600
It's a direct violation of the International Quartet's roadmap.

52
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:48,200
It's a direct violation of the commitments that leaders of Israel have made in the past at Camp David when I was president and in Oslo, promising that Israel would withdraw from occupied territories.

53
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:49,240
They have failed to do so.

54
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:53,660
In response to that, and I'm not excusing them, there have been acts of violence.

55
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:54,680
As a matter of fact, though.

